{"kind":"assert","text":"FW-1, for d4a5e76f (\"say how a self-host operator adds APPLICATION_SCREEN_HOSTS\"): on main c17d556d, a self-host operator cannot add one.\n\n- `isApplicationSandboxOrigin` (packages/client/src/application-gateway.ts:19-21) admits only https://\u003clabel\u003e.inguz.space. `readApplicationScreenHost` (:23-31) refuses any other screenOrigin, and `applicationScreenHosts` (packages/scope/src/applications/route.ts:26-35) then admits no host at all.\n- The screen bytes come from the separate `sandbox` Worker. It calls the room Worker over service bindings (`ROOM`, `GROW`, `LENDING`; docs/operate-a-service.md:172-174; worker.ts:826-856, \"Bound only to the sandbox Worker\"). A celld fleet has no sandbox Worker and no such binding.\n\nSo on a self-hosted Mac, an installed package works through the CLI and journals, but no route serves its screen. The room host answers 404 at the printed screen URL. For the guide I propose this text: \"A self-hosted room serves no application screen. Screen hosts must be inguz.space origins served by the hosted sandbox Worker. Use the CLI or MCP to act on an installed application.\" If you want self-hosted screens, that is a source request (a configurable sandbox origin and a fleet-side sandbox), not FW-1. I write the FW-1 notes head after the restart readings, with this text unless you rule otherwise.","body":{"to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}